    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" expanded="true" toc="false" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2024</fr:year><fr:month>11</fr:month><fr:day>17</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Measurability as agreement of inner and outer measure">Measurability as agreement of inner and outer measure</fr:title><fr:taxon>Exercise</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="number">3.2</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> be a probability measure on a field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathscr {F}_{0}]]></fr:tex>, define <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^{\star }(A)]]></fr:tex> for every subset <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Omega ]]></fr:tex> by <fr:link href="/000T/" title="Outer Measure" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/000T/" display-uri="000T" type="local">(3.1)</fr:link>, and denote also by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> the extension of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathscr {F}=\sigma (\mathscr {F}_{0})]]></fr:tex>. Show that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <fr:link href="/000U/" title="P^*-measurable" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/000U/" display-uri="000U" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*]]></fr:tex>-measurable</fr:link> if and only if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*(A) = P_*(A)]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>

  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1em 0 1em 0.25em;
  padding: 0.5em 1.1em 0.5em 1.2em;
  border-left: 4px solid rgba(190, 50, 130, 0.85);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  background-color: rgba(190, 50, 130, 0.06);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 600; color: rgba(190, 50, 130, 0.85);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-weight: 600; color: rgba(190, 50, 130, 0.85);">Solution.</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:p>Suppose <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*(A) = P_*(A)]]></fr:tex> and fix <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[E \subset  \Omega ]]></fr:tex>. We must show</html:p>
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[P^*(E \cap  A) + P^*(E \cap  A^c) = P^*(E)]]></fr:tex>
  <html:p>The preceding part of the exercise supplies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\overline {A}, \underline {A} \in  \mathscr {F}]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\underline {A} \subset  A \subset  \overline {A}]]></fr:tex>, and the hypothesis collapses their measures onto each other:</html:p>
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[P(\overline {A}) = P^*(A) = P_*(A) = P(\underline {A})]]></fr:tex>
  <html:p>Approximate <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[E]]></fr:tex> the same way — there is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B \in  \mathscr {F}]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[E \subset  B]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*(E) = P(B)]]></fr:tex>. Then</html:p>
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[P^*(E \cap  A) \leq  P^*(B \cap  \overline {A}) = P(B \cap  \overline {A})]]></fr:tex>
  <html:p>and</html:p>
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[P^*(B \cap  A) \leq  P^*(B \cap  \underline {A}^c) = P(B \cap  \underline {A}^c)]]></fr:tex>
  <html:p>so that</html:p>
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[P^*(E \cap  A) + P^*(E \cap  A^c) \leq  P(B \cap  \underline {A}^c) + P(B \cap  \overline {A}) = P^*(E)]]></fr:tex>
  <html:p>as desired.</html:p>

  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1em 0 1em 0.25em;
  padding: 0.5em 1.1em 0.5em 1.2em;
  border-left: 4px solid rgba(90, 90, 110, 0.7);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  background-color: rgba(90, 90, 110, 0.05);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 600; color: rgba(90, 90, 110, 0.7);">Remark.</html:span> The difficulty is bookkeeping, not measure theory: three objects live on three different domains, and conflating them is what makes the problem look impenetrable.
  <html:ol><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> is defined on the initial field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathscr {F}_0]]></fr:tex>, the closure over finite unions and complements of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Omega ]]></fr:tex>.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*]]></fr:tex> is defined on the whole powerset <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}(\Omega )]]></fr:tex>, but is <html:strong>not</html:strong> a measure there. In particular <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[E]]></fr:tex> need not lie in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathscr {F}]]></fr:tex>, which is exactly why the argument has to route through <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> (abusing notation) is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*]]></fr:tex> restricted to the <fr:link href="/000U/" title="P^*-measurable" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/000U/" display-uri="000U" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*]]></fr:tex>-measurable</fr:link> subsets of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Omega ]]></fr:tex>.</html:li></html:ol>
  The tempting shortcut — apply finite additivity and conclude everything is <fr:link href="/000U/" title="P^*-measurable" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/000U/" display-uri="000U" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*]]></fr:tex>-measurable</fr:link> — fails because <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*]]></fr:tex> is countably subadditive only on the <fr:link href="/000U/" title="P^*-measurable" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/000U/" display-uri="000U" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P^*]]></fr:tex>-measurable</fr:link> sets, not on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}(\Omega )]]></fr:tex>.</html:div>

</html:details>
</html:div>


</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  
</fr:mainmatter>
