                <fr:title text="Borel's Normal Number Theorem">Borel's Normal Number Theorem</fr:title>
                <fr:meta name="number">1</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
  
    
    
    
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" expanded="true" toc="false" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2024</fr:year><fr:month>11</fr:month><fr:day>17</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="No infinite fair independent sequence on a discrete space">No infinite fair independent sequence on a discrete space</fr:title><fr:taxon>Exercise</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="number">1.1</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>Show that a discrete probability space cannot carry an infinite sequence of independent events <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A_1, A_2, \ldots ]]></fr:tex>, each of probability <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1/2]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>

  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1em 0 1em 0.25em;
  padding: 0.5em 1.1em 0.5em 1.2em;
  border-left: 4px solid rgba(190, 50, 130, 0.85);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  background-color: rgba(190, 50, 130, 0.06);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 600; color: rgba(190, 50, 130, 0.85);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-weight: 600; color: rgba(190, 50, 130, 0.85);">Solution.</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:p>Denote the sample space <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {X}]]></fr:tex>. Independence of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A_1]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A_2]]></fr:tex> splits it into four cells of probability <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1/4]]></fr:tex> apiece:</html:p>
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[P(A_1 \cap  A_2) + P(A_1 \cap  A_2^c) + P(A_1^c \cap  A_2) + P(A_1^c \cap  A_2^c) = 1]]></fr:tex>
  <html:p>Every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x \in  \mathcal {X}]]></fr:tex> lies in exactly one of the four, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {P}(\{x\}) \leq  1/4]]></fr:tex>. Running the same construction on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A_1, \ldots , A_n]]></fr:tex> gives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> disjoint cells and the bound <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {P}(\{x\}) \leq  1/n]]></fr:tex> for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x]]></fr:tex>; the sequence is infinite, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> is unbounded and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {P}(\{x\}) = 0]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
  <html:p>But the space is discrete, so all of its mass sits on its atoms:</html:p>
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\sum _{x \in  \mathcal {X}} \mathbb {P}(\{x\}) = 1]]></fr:tex>
  <html:p>A countable sum of zeros cannot be one, and we have our contradiction.</html:p>
</html:details>
</html:div>


</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  
</fr:mainmatter>
